WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
An eval score is an estimate, not a fact.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:04.461
Last week you built graders.

3
00:00:04.741 --> 00:00:12.165
This week you use them to answer the question every team asks after a change: is the new prompt (or model) actually better?

4
00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:19.699
Suppose prompt A passes 164 of 200 questions and prompt B passes 156.

5
00:00:19.979 --> 00:00:23.477
The dashboard says 82 percent against 78 percent.

6
00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:32.717
Before anyone ships, you need to know whether a 4-point gap is a property of the prompts or an accident of which 200 questions you happened to write.

7
00:00:32.997 --> 00:00:51.856
Evan Miller’s paper “Adding Error Bars to Evals” (Anthropic, 2024) frames this the way a statistician would: the questions in your eval are a sample from a much larger population of questions you could have written, and the quantity you care about is the expected score over that population.

8
00:00:52.136 --> 00:00:55.293
Your eval score is an estimate of it, with sampling error.

9
00:00:55.573 --> 00:01:05.195
Miller’s one-line summary is that evaluations are experiments, and the eval literature has mostly ignored how other sciences analyze experiments.

10
00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:08.141
There are two sources of randomness to keep apart.

11
00:01:08.421 --> 00:01:13.840
The first is which questions you drew; it exists even for a perfectly deterministic model.

12
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:22.141
The second is the model’s own sampling: ask the same question twice and you can get two different answers (Studio 1, lesson 1).

13
00:01:22.421 --> 00:01:25.984
Both inflate the spread of your score, and they are handled differently.

14
00:01:26.264 --> 00:01:40.664
This lesson gives you the tools for each, in the order you’ll use them: an interval for one score, an interval for a difference, then the corrections for clustered questions and repeated runs, and finally how many questions you need in the first place.
